# GPU packages on the image

The software path stays installed. A supported GPU uses these packages when the
render node is present. VirtualBox has no render node, so Lavapipe remains the
Vulkan device there.

| Package | What it is |
|---|---|
| libdrm 2.4.124 | `libdrm.so.2` and `libdrm_amdgpu.so.1`. AMDGPU ioctls become R5 GPU calls |
| mesa_zink 25.3.6 | EGL vendor. llvmpipe by default. `GALLIUM_DRIVER=zink` selects Zink. Provides `mesa` |
| mesa_lavapipe 25.3.6 | Vulkan software driver |
| mesa_radv 25.3.6 | `libvulkan_radeon.so` and `add-ons/vulkan/icd.d/radeon_icd.json` |
| vulkan_loader 1.4.367 | Khronos loader. Haiku Project package |
| VulkanTeapot | Offscreen Newell teapot. `--selftest` prints the device and sample pixels |

RADV is not the default for GC 9.3. The ring, the workload, and compute stay
off until the radeon_hd driver settings say so.

The package is named `mesa`, version `25.3.6-2`. It provides `mesa`,
`mesa_zink` and `mesa_eglvnd`. `haiku/build/jam/BuildFeatures` fetches
`file: base mesa`. `mesa-25.3.6-1` is not in the pin and not in the download
directory: it owns the same EGL file. `mesa_devel` and `mesa_lavapipe` require
`mesa == 25.3.6`.

DRM version reported to RADV is 3.42.0. That is the oldest version
`ac_gpu_info.c` accepts. Every `drm_minor >= 45` gate in that file stays off,
including gang submit, GPUVM faults and conformant trunc. Sparse binding
stays advertised. It is not turned off by lowering this version.

`GEM_VA` `MAP` with handle 0 is valid only with `AMDGPU_VM_PAGE_PRT`. The
kernel writes a PRT page-table entry: Linux `AMDGPU_PTE_PRT` is bit 51 on
GC 9.3 (`kR5Gfx9PtePrt`), and `VALID` stays clear. `gmc_v9_0_get_vm_pte`
does that and writes no further GMC 9 register for the mapping.
`REPLACE` clears the entries in the range and then maps the buffer, or
writes PRT again when the handle is 0 and the PRT flag is set. `CLEAR`
writes empty entries, the way `amdgpu_vm_bo_clear_mappings` removes the
interval. A second team still gets `B_NOT_ALLOWED` until M5.

A client wait that times out returns `B_TIMED_OUT` and leaves the device
usable. The retire thread marks the device lost when the oldest job has
been in the ring for 10 seconds (`kWatchdogUs`), the GFX value of
`amdgpu.lockup_timeout`. Closing a buffer does not
wait: the buffer stays reserved until that job retires, or until the
watchdog. There is no reset in M4.

A binary syncobj follows the latest submit. It is signalled when `submitted`
is greater than 0 and `point` has caught up with `submitted`. `submitted` 0
means it was never submitted, or it was reset. A job signals it with that
job's sequence, never with 1. A timeline signal whose point is 0 is that
same sequence: RADV's queue syncobj is created with `CREATE_SIGNALED` and
then signaled at point 0. `submitted` rises when the job is queued.
`point` rises when that job's sequence is on the user fence page. The ring
read pointer and the act of submitting do not finish the job.
`CREATE_SIGNALED` sets both to 1. A host signal sets both to the greater of
the current values and the highest sequence, or 1 when no job has run. A
transfer or a wait on a sync that a live job still lists waits for that
job. `SYNCOBJ_DESTROY` detaches the sync from any live job and succeeds.
The job does not signal a sync that has been destroyed. `DRM_IOCTL_SYNCOBJ_HANDLE_TO_FD`, `FD_TO_HANDLE` and
`PRIME` return ENOSYS. `SYNCOBJ_TRANSFER` is `R5_GPU_SYNCOBJ_TRANSFER`.
Any other untranslated ioctl returns ENOSYS and is
named once on stderr. `GEM_MMAP` is one of those: CPU mapping uses `BO_MAP`.

Compute is reported with `available_rings` 0. A command submission whose
`ip_type` is not GFX, or whose IB flags are not 0, returns EINVAL. VMID 1 is
one shared address space. `CS` passes `ctx_id`. `GEM_VA` in libdrm 2.4.124
has no context field, so that call uses context 1. A dependency chunk waits
on that sequence for five seconds and is not dropped. `WAIT_CS` uses the same
fence wait. The submit wait is five seconds and returns a timeout.

`GET_INFO` reports `high_va_offset` `0xffff800000000000` and `high_va_max`
`0xffffffffffbfe000`, in the common block and in the AMD vendor block.
That is the range `amdgpu_kms.c` reports for this VM. libdrm then keeps
`AMDGPU_VA_RANGE_HIGH`, and RADV places 32-bit buffers at `address32_hi`
(`0xffff800000000000`). A map in that range uses the existing 4-level
page tables. U4 (VRAM) and U2 (GTT) run at the end of stage P, and again
in stage U. The first 16 `CS_SUBMIT` calls log `submit ib va … dw …`.

The host replay (`ok libdrm ioctl`, then `replay peaks bo 1 sync 10`) used one
buffer and ten syncobjs. The kernel tables are 256 buffers, 256 buffers per
client, 128 syncobjs and 64 jobs. A full RADV device was not run.

## VRAM and GTT

`GET_INFO` sets `vram_bytes` from `shared_info->frame_buffer_size` times 1024. That field is KiB. `gtt_bytes` is the same number. It is not a quarter of system memory.

On Tahiti and later, including the 5625U, `CONFIG_MEMSIZE` is read as megabytes. A value of 0 or above 65536 MB is ignored. Raven leaves the register at `0x3fffff`, so it is ignored. `graphics_memory_size` stays 0 and `frame_buffer_size` becomes the PCI framebuffer BAR, in KiB. The driver does not read the UMA carveout.

The carveout on the 5625U is 512 MiB. Windows shows 495 MB for that reservation. The number `GET_INFO` returns is the PCI BAR, which can be larger than the carveout.

| Call | Result |
|---|---|
| `drmGetDevices2` / `drmGetDevice2` | translated, PCI from `GET_INFO` |
| `drmGetVersion` | translated, 3.42.0 |
| `AMDGPU_INFO_DEV_INFO` | translated |
| `AMDGPU_INFO_HW_IP` GFX | translated, rings 1 |
| `AMDGPU_INFO_HW_IP` compute | translated, rings 0 |
| `AMDGPU_INFO_FW_VERSION` ME, MEC, PFP | translated, from the ucode header |
| `AMDGPU_INFO_MEMORY` | translated, max allocation 64 MiB |
| `AMDGPU_INFO_VRAM_GTT` | translated |
| `AMDGPU_INFO_READ_MMR_REG` `0x263e` | translated as `0x063e` |
| `DRM_CAP_SYNCOBJ` and `DRM_CAP_SYNCOBJ_TIMELINE` | translated, value 1 |
| syncobj create, reset, signal, query | translated |
| syncobj wait, one list, one timeout | translated. A timeout is `ETIME`. Without `WAIT_ALL` the first signalled handle wins. `WAIT_AVAILABLE` waits for the submitted point. Without `WAIT_FOR_SUBMIT` an unsubmitted syncobj returns `EINVAL` instead of a timeout |
| `SYNCOBJ_HANDLE_TO_FD`, `FD_TO_HANDLE` | ENOSYS on purpose |
| `SYNCOBJ_TRANSFER` | translated. `src_point` 0 is the binary fence and is reached only when that syncobj is signalled. A reached source signals the destination the way a host signal does. Otherwise the live job that brings the source to its latest value gets the destination: `job->sequence` when `dst_point` is 0, otherwise `dst_point`, and `submitted` rises at once. No such job is `EINVAL` (`drm_syncobj_find_fence` in Linux 6.12 returns `-EINVAL` when the syncobj has no fence). A missing handle is `ENOENT`. A full job list is `ENOMEM`. `flags` and `pad` must be 0. Sync-file export and import stay ENOSYS |
| `PRIME` | ENOSYS on purpose |
| `CS` binary `chunk_sem` | translated |
| `CS` timeline chunk | translated |
| `CS` dependencies | translated, fence wait before submit |
| `WAIT_CS` | translated |
| `GEM_MMAP` | ENOSYS on purpose. CPU mapping is `BO_MAP`. RADV calls `r5_bo_cpu_map` instead of this ioctl |

`BO_MAP` clones the kernel buffer into the calling team with `vm_clone_area(..., B_READ_AREA | B_WRITE_AREA, REGION_NO_PRIVATE_MAP, ..., kernel = true)`. The kernel does not read or write that clone. `get_memory_map_etc` reads the kernel area only. The user bits stay in the program's team, so SMAP is not asked to accept user pages in the kernel address space. A second `BO_MAP` from that team returns the same area. Another team gets `B_NOT_ALLOWED` until M5. The kernel deletes the clone with `vm_delete_area(team, area, true)` when the buffer is released, and does nothing when that team has already ended. The source area is not `B_CLONEABLE_AREA`. libdrm does not clone it, and it does not delete the user clone on unmap.

`VA_MAP` of a GTT buffer uses `get_memory_map_etc`. Haiku's `get_memory_map` returns `B_OK` and a size-0 end entry, so it covers no pages. More than 256 physical pieces continues; a piece that still does not cover the range is `B_BAD_DATA` (`refuse VA_MAP short`).

A user GTT page-table entry is `MTYPE_NC` and `SNOOPED`. Linux v6.12 `amdgpu_bo_support_uswc()` (`drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/amdgpu_object.c`) returns false on the path air/OS uses, and `AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE_CPU_GTT_USWC` is then ignored: every GTT buffer is cached and snooped. R5 takes that mode. `AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE_CPU_GTT_USWC` is still `R5_GPU_BO_WC` and the kernel stores the bit, but no RAM area is given a write-combined memory type, and the page-table entry is the cached one (`r5_gfx9_user_pte` with write-combined 0). The reply flags have that bit clear. `BO_CREATE` prints `bo domain`, the requested flags, the used flags and `snooped 1` while `gpu_selftest` is on. libdrm prints `kernel bo domain` with those used flags. Linux v6.12 `gmc_v9_0_get_coherence_flags` (the branch that is not GC 9.4) uses `MTYPE_NC` and then sets `SNOOPED` on every system page. `ttm_tt_pte_flags` leaves `SNOOPED` clear for `ttm_write_combined`; that entry is not used while this mode is on. The same Linux function maps user VRAM that is not uncached as `MTYPE_NC`. That VRAM entry stays `MTYPE_UC` until a boot compares it with the page that already draws. The row is `kR5Gfx93Mtype` (`vram_live` 0). The K2 page and the kernel GART stay `MTYPE_UC`. The VRAM window is unchanged. A write-combined RAM type comes back only with a PAT-only path, and not in M4. Before a GTT indirect buffer is submitted the kernel `clflush`es that range and then `mfence`. The address is the kernel area. This flush stays until a boot shows the snoop. `CP_IB1_BASE_HI` is 16 bits in the hardware mask as well, so the packet still masks the high half with `0xffff`. A chained indirect buffer uses the same domain and the same packet.

Three log keys, read from the radeon_hd settings file. Errors are always on, and at most 32 of them in a boot: `refuse`, `syncobj limit`, `gpu lost` with the indirect-buffer dump and the PM4 lines of that dump, and the non-EOP interrupt lines (a VM fault, `PRIV_REG`, `PRIV_INSTR`, an opcode error). `gpu_debug true` (default off) prints `submit ib va`, `va map` with the address, the size and the domain, the per-call lines such as `vmid1 reused`, and the translator's successful ioctl line. `gpu_selftest true` prints the stage bo/P/U lines, the self-test indirect buffer, and the teapot stages. `rb num` is printed once per boot. The fixed K sequence logs as it does now.

`R5_GPU_SELFTEST` (`R5_GPU_OP_BASE + 23`) runs the VMID 1 steps when `gpu_selftest` is true. Otherwise it is `B_NOT_ALLOWED`. The first open of the render node does not run them. Stage U prints the variable MTRRs before the steps and after them. U-self stores eight words on one cached GTT page and one VRAM page, reads them through that pointer and through a second mapping of the same physical page, and stops the series if they differ. A new leaf table is `0x10`. A new directory page is `AMDGPU_PDE_PTE`. An unmap writes `0x10`, and a PRT entry stays. Then U2a-n (VRAM indirect buffer, fresh cached GTT, CPU read without `clflush`), U2a-f (the same read with `clflush`), U2b-n (cached GTT indirect buffer, CPU write without `clflush`, VRAM destination), U4b-pair (VRAM at `0xffff800000001000` and the next page), then VRAM at `0xffffffffffbfc000`, `0xffff800100000000`, `0xffff808000000000`, `0xffff800040000000`, `0xffff800000200000`, low `0x100000`, and U4b. Before each of those submits the four table words are read through a fresh mapping of the table page. There is no write-combined step and no `-lx` step. Each step has its own virtual address. A miss continues. A busy command processor at 5 seconds stops the series. The `clflush` on `CS_SUBMIT` stays. The line before the submit prints the first eight dwords and the physical address of the indirect-buffer page.

These `AMDGPU_INFO` queries stay ENOSYS on purpose. RADV 25.3.6 does not need them to create a device:

| Query | RADV 25.3.6 |
|---|---|
| `0x05` `TIMESTAMP` | `query_value` returns 0 |
| `0x0f` `NUM_BYTES_MOVED` | `query_value` returns 0 |
| `0x18` `NUM_EVICTIONS` | `query_value` returns 0 |
| `0x1d` `SENSOR` (clocks and temperature) | `query_value` returns 0 |
| `0x1e` `NUM_VRAM_CPU_PAGE_FAULTS` | `query_value` returns 0 |
| `0x21` `VIDEO_CAPS` decode and encode | a zeroed `drm_amdgpu_info_video_caps`. That is the Linux answer for a chip without the codec. Any other subquery is `EINVAL`. There is no video block |
| `0x23` `GPUVM_FAULT` | the winsys prints a line and reports no fault |

`0x10` `VRAM_USAGE`, `0x11` `GTT_USAGE` and `0x17` `VIS_VRAM_USAGE` are the live buffer bytes. Visible VRAM equals VRAM: the whole BAR is CPU-visible. `0x22` `MAX_IBS` is 8 for GFX and 0 for every other IP. The kernel accepts 8 indirect buffers on the GFX ring.

A failed `AMDGPU_INFO` line includes `query 0x..`.

## Error signs on Haiku

POSIX codes are negative. `return -errno` becomes positive. `r == -ETIME`, `-ECANCELED` and `-ENOMEM` still match that positive value, so the `return -errno` sites in `xf86drm.c` and `amdgpu_*.c` stay as they are. `r5_neg()` is only on returns Mesa does not compare with a specific `-E` code.

| Return | Caller | Comparison | `r5_neg` |
|---|---|---|---|
| `r5_drm_get_devices2` missing directory returns 0 | `vk_instance.c:418` | `< 1` | no. A negated errno was a device count |
| `r5_drm_get_device2` | device match | `!= 0` and `< 0` in the host test | yes. No `== -E` in Mesa |
| `r5_bo_cpu_map` / `unmap` | `amdgpu_bo_cpu_map` returns that value. `radv_amdgpu_bo.c:730` tests it with `if (ac_drm_bo_cpu_map(...))` | any non-zero | yes |
| `drmSyncobjWait` / `TimelineWait` `xf86drm.c:5042` and `:5109` | `vk_drm_syncobj.c` | `== -ETIME` and `errno == ETIME` | no. libdrm keeps `-errno` |
| `amdgpu_cs.c` submit `== -ENOMEM`, `-ECANCELED`, `-ETIME` | `radv_amdgpu_cs.c:1966`–`:1984` | exact code | no |

`drm_ioctl` in Mesa `os_drm.h` also returns `-errno`. `ac_drm_query_info` uses it. The syncobj helpers `drmSyncobjCreate`, `Transfer`, `ExportSyncFile`, `ImportSyncFile` and `Query2` return `drmIoctl`, which is `-1`.

| Test | Value | Change |
|---|---|---|
| `vk_drm_syncobj.c:59`, `:67`, `:663` | `drmSyncobjCreate` / timeline signal, `drmIoctl` returns `-1` | left |
| `vk_drm_syncobj.c:498` | `sync_merge`, not a libdrm `-errno` | left |
| `radv_amdgpu_cs.c:1280`, `:1286`, `:1298`, `:1318`, `:1330`, `:1335`, `:1340`, `:1347` | export, import and transfer return `drmIoctl`'s `-1` | left |
| `radv_amdgpu_winsys.c:106` | `ac_drm_query_info` returns `-errno` | `!= 0`. Patch `distro/mesa/patches/haiku-errno-nonzero.patch` |

The error mapper stays out. Its hidden `_errnop` writes a positive code into the thread's errno cell, which VulkanTeapot and libbe share.
